      "current_assessment": "Western Europe delivered the densest single-week evidence in this dynamic's tracking history: all four reporting countries record governing legitimacy crises crossing qualitative new thresholds simultaneously. Germany's 83% Merz disapproval (worst recorded for an incumbent chancellor) and Kanzlertausch erupting publicly with five senior CDU/CSU figures required for emergency consolidation. Britain's Blair 5,700-word diagnosis of 'no coherent plan' paired with Burnham succession preparations and Parliament compelling Mandelson disclosure. Italy's Crosetto-Meloni public rupture over SAFE financing, with Meloni's 2027 majority now mathematically dependent on Vannacci reintegration. Spain's UCO searching PSOE headquarters for the first time in democratic Spanish history plus the David Sánchez trial opening. In CEE, Babiš's explicit Trump-alignment justification for NATO non-compliance and the Pavel-Babiš constitutional standoff are producing internationally legible diplomatic incoherence at a NATO ally. The mechanisms remain entirely distinct — German leadership speculation, British succession dynamics, Italian coalition fragmentation, Spanish judicial cascade — but they share structural drivers: Iran-war energy cost transmission (Panetta projects 6% eurozone inflation), fiscal squeeze from defense commitments compounding post-COVID consolidation, and US policy unpredictability generating foreign policy stress that compounds domestic political costs.",
