      "status": "developing",
      "current_assessment": "The 1st German/Netherlands Corps (Münster) formally assuming tactical command responsibility for NATO forces in Estonia and Latvia by mid-year 2026 is the positive resilience development of the week — a structural improvement to Baltic deterrence architecture that is European-driven and US-independent, giving Estonia and Latvia a dedicated corps-level command (capacity for 40,000-60,000 troops across 16 nations) that enables 'mass at speed' reinforcement previously impossible from the single Szczecin headquarters. Latvia, Estonia, and Lithuania all covered the development, with Latvia's new Defence Minister Melnis citing it in his first-week priorities. However, the limits side is equally documented: the 1GNC command structure's operational value depends on forces available to flow through it — precisely what concurrent US asset withdrawal reports threaten. Romania's SAFE unsigned status means the corps's most immediate potential beneficiary on the kinetic-contact flank cannot procure the interceptors the command structure would direct. Czech non-compliance is removing the ammunition foundation from the eastern flank's conventional deterrent. The gap between improved command architecture and degraded capability availability — better plumbing, thinner water — is the dynamic's defining tension this week.",
